W8 = "Note: these lessons are based only on the publisher's description of The Wellness 8 [VERIFY]. They explain a general wellness model in our own words and do not claim to reproduce what the book says."
L = {}
L['w8-more-than-health'] = dict(
intro="Most of us learn to think about health in a simple way: if nothing hurts and the doctor has no complaints, we must be fine. Yet many people know the odd feeling of being 'not ill' and still not feeling well. This lesson looks at the broader idea of wellness that sits behind The Wellness 8 as its publisher describes it, and behind OneUP's own eight dimensions. " + W8,
sections=[
("Health and wellness are not the same word", "Health is often described by what is missing: no fever, no injury, no worrying test result. Wellness, in the way many wellness models use the word, is described by what is present: energy for the day, relationships that feel supportive, work that feels meaningful, a sense of calm about money and surroundings. The two overlap a great deal, but they are not identical. Someone can have a clean bill of health and still feel flat, lonely or stuck. Someone else can be living with a long-term condition and still feel that many parts of life are going well.\n\nThinking in terms of wellness does not replace medical care. It simply widens the lens so that you notice more of your life at once."),
("Why the whole picture matters", "Areas of life tend to lean on each other. Poor sleep can make patience thin, which can strain a friendship, which can make it harder to focus at work. Money worries can quietly affect sleep. A cluttered room can make it harder to rest. Because of these links, improving one area sometimes eases another, and neglecting one area can drag others down.\n\nA whole-life view helps you avoid a common trap: pouring all your effort into one area, such as fitness or career, while other areas slowly wear thin without you noticing."),
("Wellness as a direction, not a finish line", "A helpful way to hold this idea is to treat wellness as something you move towards rather than something you either have or lack. Every area has good weeks and hard weeks. The aim is not to score perfectly everywhere, which nobody does, but to notice where you are and choose a small next step.\n\nThis framing is gentler. It replaces 'Am I healthy or not?' with 'Which part of my life could use a little care right now?' That question is easier to act on, and it leaves room for the fact that life changes."),
("What this does not mean", "A wider view of wellness is not a diagnosis tool, and it is not a reason to ignore symptoms. If something in your body or mood worries you, a qualified professional is the right person to talk to. A wellness model is a way to reflect and plan everyday habits, nothing more and nothing less."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Meera's 'fine' month", "Meera, a software tester in Pune, had a routine check-up and every result came back normal. She was relieved, yet she still felt drained most evenings. When she looked at her life area by area, a pattern appeared. Her body was fine, but she had not called her close friends in weeks, her desk was buried under papers, and she felt uneasy because she had no idea where her salary went each month. None of these was a medical problem. Together, they explained why 'healthy' did not feel like 'well'. She chose one small step, a Sunday call with a friend, and noticed the week felt lighter. This is an illustrative story, not a real case."),
takeaways=["Health often means the absence of illness; wellness looks at what is present in your life.","Areas of life influence each other, so a whole-life view catches problems early.","Treat wellness as a direction you move in, not a test you pass or fail.","A wellness model supports reflection; it does not replace professional care."],
reflect=["When did you last feel 'healthy' but not 'well'? What was missing?","Which area of your life tends to get ignored when you are busy?"],
)
L['w8-wheel'] = dict(
intro="Picture a bicycle wheel. When every spoke is strong, the ride is smooth. When a few spokes are loose, the wheel wobbles even if the rest are fine. Many wellness models use a wheel like this to show how different parts of life hold each other up. The publisher describes The Wellness 8 as being organised around eight dimensions of wellness [VERIFY exact list], and OneUP is organised around eight dimensions as well. " + W8,
sections=[
("The eight spokes in OneUP", "OneUP uses eight dimensions: physical, emotional, social, spiritual, intellectual, occupational, environmental and financial. Physical covers the body: movement, rest and food. Emotional covers how you notice and handle feelings. Social is about connection and belonging. Spiritual is about meaning, values and what feels bigger than daily tasks, whether or not that involves religion. Intellectual is about curiosity and learning. Occupational is about work, study or any role that fills your days. Environmental is about the spaces you live and work in. Financial is about your relationship with money and the sense of security around it.\n\nDifferent books and organisations name and group these areas slightly differently. The labels matter less than the habit of looking at all of them."),
("Why a wheel and not a ladder", "A ladder suggests you climb one rung at a time and leave the lower ones behind. A wheel suggests that every part keeps mattering at once. You never 'finish' the physical spoke and move on to the social one. Instead, attention shifts around the wheel as life changes. During exams, the intellectual spoke may take most of your energy. After a move to a new city, the social and environmental spokes may need more care."),
("Balance does not mean equal", "It is tempting to think a balanced wheel means spending the same time on every area. In practice that is neither possible nor necessary. Balance here means that no spoke is so neglected that it makes the whole ride wobble. A busy parent might give very little time to hobbies for a while, and that can be perfectly fine, as long as it is a conscious choice and not a slow drift.\n\nA quick self-check, rating each area from one to five, can show which spoke is loosest. That loosest spoke is often the best place for a small next step."),
("Using the wheel without judging yourself", "Low scores are information, not a verdict on your worth. Everyone has areas that are harder for them, and those can change from month to month. The point of the wheel is to guide attention kindly, not to create another list of things to feel guilty about."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Arjun's loose spoke", "Arjun, a final-year engineering student in Chennai, rated himself on all eight dimensions one evening. Intellectual and occupational were high because of placements. Physical was middling. Social was a one: he had stopped joining his friends for evening tea since exams began. He realised the loneliness was making him more anxious about results. He did not quit studying; he simply joined the tea break twice a week. The wheel did not tell him what to do. It showed him where to look. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["A wellness wheel shows that areas of life support each other.","OneUP uses eight dimensions; other models group them slightly differently.","Balance means no area is badly neglected, not that every area gets equal time.","Low scores point to where a small step may help most."],
reflect=["If you rated each of the eight areas from one to five today, which would be lowest?","Is that low area a conscious trade-off right now, or a slow drift?"],
)
L['w8-learning'] = dict(
intro="Learning is easy to associate with school and college, something that ends when a degree certificate arrives. Yet curiosity does not have an expiry date. The publisher's description of The Wellness 8 includes ongoing learning as part of a well life [VERIFY]. This lesson explores, in general terms, why intellectual wellness matters even when life is busy. " + W8,
sections=[
("What intellectual wellness means", "Intellectual wellness is about keeping your mind engaged. That can mean reading, picking up a skill, asking questions, solving puzzles, having a good debate, or exploring a subject purely for the joy of it. It is not about being clever or having degrees. A grandmother learning to video-call her grandchildren is exercising this dimension just as much as a researcher writing a paper."),
("Why learning feels good", "Many people notice that learning something new brings a quiet sense of satisfaction. It can make the days feel less repetitive, give you fresh things to talk about, and build confidence that you can handle unfamiliar situations. Learning also tends to spill into other dimensions. A new cooking skill touches the physical dimension. A course related to your job touches the occupational one. Learning a little about budgeting touches the financial one."),
("The busy-life problem", "The usual reason people stop learning is not lack of interest but lack of time and energy. After work, commuting and household duties, a long course can feel impossible. The answer is usually to make learning smaller rather than to give it up. Ten minutes of reading before bed, a podcast during a commute, or one new recipe a week all count. Small, regular learning often adds up to more than occasional bursts of enthusiasm that fade."),
("Learning with others", "Learning does not have to be solitary. Discussing a book with a friend, joining a local class, or teaching a younger relative something you know can make learning more enjoyable and help it stick. Explaining an idea to someone else is also a good way to find out how well you really understand it."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Lakshmi's commute library", "Lakshmi spends about forty minutes each way on a Mumbai local train. For months she scrolled social media on the journey and arrived home feeling scattered. She decided to try one change: on the morning ride she would listen to an episode of an educational podcast about history, a subject she had loved in school. She did not set any target. After a few weeks, she found she was looking forward to the commute and chatting with her teenage son about what she had heard. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Intellectual wellness is about curiosity and engagement, not intelligence or qualifications.","Learning can lift mood and spill into other dimensions of life.","When time is short, make learning smaller instead of stopping it.","Learning with or teaching others makes it more enjoyable and memorable."],
reflect=["What is something you were curious about as a child that you have not explored in years?","Where in your week is there a small pocket of time that could hold ten minutes of learning?"],
)
L['w8-kindness'] = dict(
intro="Kindness is often described as something we do for others. It can also be part of how we look after our own wellbeing. The publisher's description of The Wellness 8 touches on kindness and contribution [VERIFY]. This lesson explores, in general terms, how giving and receiving kindness connects to social and emotional wellness. " + W8,
sections=[
("Kindness as connection", "Every act of kindness, however small, creates a moment of connection. Holding a door, thanking the security guard by name, sending a message to a friend who has had a tough week: these small acts remind both people that they are not alone. Social wellness grows from many such moments, not only from big gatherings or close friendships."),
("Why giving can feel good", "Many people notice that helping someone else lifts their own mood. Doing something for another person can shift attention away from our own worries for a while and give a sense of purpose. That feeling is a natural part of being a social creature. It is worth noticing, though, that kindness offered only to feel good about ourselves can become a performance. The most nourishing kindness tends to come from genuine care."),
("Kindness that includes yourself", "Kindness is not only outward. The way you speak to yourself after a mistake matters too. Many people are far gentler with friends than with themselves. Practising the same patience inward, such as saying 'That was hard, and I did my best' instead of harsh self-criticism, is part of emotional wellness. Self-kindness is not the same as making excuses; it simply means not adding extra pain to a difficult moment."),
("Kindness with boundaries", "Being kind does not mean saying yes to everything. Constantly giving without rest can lead to resentment and exhaustion. Healthy kindness includes limits: helping when you can, saying no honestly when you cannot, and accepting help from others in return. Receiving kindness gracefully is also a gift to the person offering it."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the building watchman", "Rohit had lived in his Bengaluru apartment block for two years without learning the night watchman's name. One evening, returning late and tired, he stopped to ask. The watchman, Suresh, told him he had a daughter starting college. Over the next months, their brief chats became something Rohit looked forward to. Nothing dramatic changed, but Rohit noticed he felt more at home in the building, and Suresh seemed to feel more seen. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Small acts of kindness create moments of connection that support social wellness.","Helping others can give a sense of purpose, especially when it comes from genuine care.","Kindness includes how you speak to yourself.","Healthy kindness has boundaries and includes receiving help too."],
reflect=["Who is someone you see often but have never really spoken to?","How do you usually talk to yourself after a mistake? Would you say the same to a friend?"],
)
L['w8-surroundings'] = dict(
intro="The spaces we spend time in, a bedroom, a desk, a kitchen, a neighbourhood, quietly shape how we feel and what we do. Many wellness models include an environmental dimension for this reason, and the publisher's description of The Wellness 8 includes environmental wellness [VERIFY]. This lesson explains the idea in general terms. " + W8,
sections=[
("What environmental wellness covers", "Environmental wellness is about your relationship with the spaces around you. At the smallest scale, it is your room or desk: is there light, air and a place to rest? At a wider scale, it includes your home, your street, access to green spaces, and how you care for the shared environment. It does not require a large or expensive home. A small, tidy corner with a plant and good light can do a lot."),
("How spaces shape behaviour", "Surroundings make some actions easier and others harder. A phone charging next to your pillow makes late-night scrolling easy. A water bottle on your desk makes drinking water easy. A cluttered dining table makes eating together harder. Because of this, changing your surroundings is often a gentler way to change habits than relying on willpower alone."),
("Spaces and mood", "Many people notice that light, noise, clutter and smell affect how they feel. A dark, messy room can make it harder to get going in the morning. Opening a window, sitting near natural light, or having a quiet corner can help some people feel calmer. Everyone is different, so the aim is to notice what helps you rather than follow a fixed rule."),
("Caring for the wider environment", "Environmental wellness also reaches outward. Small actions such as reducing waste, caring for a shared park, or carrying a cloth bag can give a sense of contribution. These actions connect personal wellbeing with the wellbeing of a neighbourhood or community."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: Fatima's study corner", "Fatima, a college student in Hyderabad, shared a room with her younger sister and studied on her bed, surrounded by clothes and chargers. She often felt sleepy and distracted. With her sister's help, she cleared one end of a shelf, placed a small lamp and a potted money plant there, and used a folding stool as a seat. It was not a perfect study room, but having one clear spot that meant 'study' made it easier to start. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Environmental wellness is about your relationship with the spaces around you.","Surroundings make some actions easier and others harder.","Light, noise and clutter can affect mood; notice what helps you.","Caring for shared spaces connects personal and community wellbeing."],
reflect=["Which space do you spend the most time in, and how does it make you feel?","What is one object you could move to make a good habit easier?"],
)
L['w8-clutter'] = dict(
intro="Clutter is not only about objects. It can be old papers, unused apps, unanswered messages, or commitments we no longer want. The publisher's description of The Wellness 8 mentions letting go of what holds you back [VERIFY]. This lesson explores, in general terms, how clearing physical and mental clutter can support environmental and emotional wellness. " + W8,
sections=[
("Why clutter weighs on us", "Each item in a space quietly asks for attention. A pile of unread papers reminds you of tasks. Clothes that no longer fit can stir guilt. Broken gadgets waiting to be repaired whisper 'someday'. Individually, these are small. Together, they can create a background sense of unfinished business that makes rest harder."),
("Physical clutter", "Clearing physical clutter does not have to mean a big weekend overhaul. A useful approach is to start with one small area, such as a drawer or a bag, and ask simple questions about each item: Do I use this? Do I need it? Would I buy it again? Items that pass can stay. Items that do not can be donated, recycled or given to someone who will use them. Many households in India already have a culture of passing things on to relatives or neighbours, which can make letting go feel meaningful rather than wasteful."),
("Digital and mental clutter", "Phones and inboxes collect clutter too: hundreds of unread notifications, group chats that no longer matter, apps that are never opened. Muting or leaving a few groups, turning off non-essential notifications and deleting unused apps can make your phone feel calmer. Mental clutter, the list of things you keep reminding yourself to do, can often be eased by writing it down so your mind does not have to hold it."),
("Letting go gently", "Some items carry emotional weight: a parent's old sari, letters from a friend, a child's first drawing. Letting go does not mean discarding memories. It can mean keeping a few meaningful pieces in a special box, photographing others, and accepting that memories live in you, not only in objects. Go at your own pace; there is no need to clear everything."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the 'someday' drawer", "Kavya had a kitchen drawer in her Kolkata flat stuffed with old bills, takeaway menus, rubber bands and dead batteries. Every time she opened it, she felt a flicker of irritation. One Sunday she gave herself fifteen minutes to empty it. She kept a few useful things, filed two important bills, and sent the batteries for recycling. The drawer now closes easily, and she noticed she reaches for it without that small sigh. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Clutter can be physical, digital or mental, and each type draws attention.","Start small: one drawer, one bag, one app folder.","Writing tasks down can ease mental clutter.","Letting go of objects does not mean letting go of memories."],
reflect=["Which space or device feels most cluttered to you right now?","Is there something you keep 'for someday' that you could pass on to someone who would use it?"],
)
L['w8-financial-freedom'] = dict(
intro="Money is one of the most common sources of worry, yet it is rarely discussed as part of wellbeing. The publisher's description of The Wellness 8 includes financial wellness [VERIFY]. This lesson explains, in general terms, how your relationship with money connects to emotional wellness. It is general learning only and not financial advice. " + W8,
sections=[
("Financial wellness is a feeling as well as a number", "Financial wellness is not only about how much money you have. Two people with similar incomes can feel very differently about money. One feels calm because they know roughly where their money goes. The other feels anxious because every month ends with surprises. Financial wellness, in the way wellness models describe it, includes a sense of awareness, some feeling of control, and the ability to talk about money without shame."),
("How money and emotions connect", "Money decisions are rarely purely logical. People spend to celebrate, to cope with a hard day, to keep up with others, or out of habit. Worry about money can affect sleep, relationships and focus at work. Noticing the emotions behind spending, without judging them, is often the first step towards a calmer relationship with money."),
("Awareness before change", "A gentle starting point is simply to look. Many people avoid checking their bank balance or card statements because it feels stressful. Yet uncertainty often feels worse than knowing. Writing down where money went over a week or a month, without trying to change anything yet, can reveal patterns. You might discover that small daily purchases add up, or that a subscription you forgot about is still running."),
("Talking about money", "In many families, money is a private or awkward topic. Yet talking openly with a partner or family member about shared goals and worries can reduce tension. When decisions are big, such as loans, investments or insurance, a qualified financial professional is the right person to consult. This lesson does not recommend any product or strategy."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the UPI surprise", "Nikhil, a young professional in Delhi, felt he was always short of money by the twentieth of each month but could not explain why. He decided to scroll through his UPI history for one month and jot down categories in a notebook. He was surprised by how many small food-delivery payments there were. He did not set strict rules; he simply became aware and chose to cook at home two more nights a week. The feeling of not knowing eased. This is an illustrative story and not financial advice."),
takeaways=["Financial wellness includes awareness and a sense of control, not just income.","Emotions often drive spending; noticing them is a useful first step.","Looking at where money goes can reduce the stress of uncertainty.","For big decisions, consult a qualified financial professional. This is not financial advice."],
reflect=["How do you feel when you check your bank balance? What does that feeling tell you?","What is one money conversation you have been avoiding?"],
)
L['w8-learning']['sections'].append(("Making it a habit", "Learning sticks best when it has a regular place in your week. You might link it to something you already do, such as reading a few pages with your morning tea, or keeping a small notebook of interesting things you came across each day. Another helpful idea is to follow your own curiosity rather than what you think you should learn. Interest makes it much easier to keep going, and keeping going matters more than the size of each session. Over months, these small sessions quietly build knowledge and confidence."))
L['w8-kindness']['sections'].append(("Starting small", "You do not need grand gestures to practise kindness. A sincere thank-you, remembering someone's name, listening without checking your phone, or sharing food with a neighbour are all forms of it. Some people find it helpful to notice one kind act they did and one they received at the end of each day. This is not about keeping score. It simply trains attention towards the warmth that is already present in ordinary life, which can be easy to overlook on a busy or difficult day."))
L['w8-surroundings']['sections'].append(("Small changes, noticeable effects", "You do not need to redecorate to improve environmental wellness. Small changes are often enough: moving a chair closer to a window, keeping one surface clear, putting a plant on a sill, or setting a basket by the door for keys and bags. Try one change at a time and notice how it feels over a week. If it helps, keep it. If not, try something else. Treating your space as an experiment, rather than a project you must finish, keeps the process light and manageable."))
L['w8-clutter']['sections'].append(("Keeping it clear", "After clearing a space, the challenge is keeping it that way. A simple habit is to give every frequently used item a 'home', so it is easy to put back. Another is a short daily reset, a few minutes in the evening to return things to their places. When new things come in, it can help to ask whether something old can go out. These are not strict rules, only gentle supports. The aim is a space that feels lighter to live in, not a perfectly empty room."))
L['w8-financial-freedom']['sections'].append(("Small steps towards calm", "Financial calm usually grows from small, steady habits rather than dramatic changes. Some people find it helpful to set a regular, short 'money moment' each week to look at what came in and went out. Others like to name their goals in plain words, such as 'feel less anxious at month end'. Noticing progress, even modest progress, can make money feel less overwhelming. Remember that everyone's situation is different, and what works for one household may not suit another. This is general learning, not financial advice."))
L['w8-surroundings']['sections'][0]=(L['w8-surroundings']['sections'][0][0], L['w8-surroundings']['sections'][0][1]+" What matters is that the space supports what you want to do there, whether that is resting, working, cooking or spending time with family.")
